The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5x^4+x^2}{2x^4-x^3-4}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5x^4+x^2}{x^{4}}}{\dfrac{2x^4-x^3-4}{x^{4}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}+\dfrac{\cancel {x^2}}{\cancel {x^2}\cdot x^2}}{\dfrac{2\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}-\dfrac{\cancel{x^3}}{x\cdot\cancel{x^3}}-\dfrac{4}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5+\dfrac{1}{x^2}}{2-\dfrac{1}{x}-\dfrac{4}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5+0}{2-0-0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5}{2}
\end{align}$
